Acquire and release locks with proper error handling:

```java
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    private final RedissonClient redisson;

    public PaymentResult processPayment(String orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Lock scoped to this specific order
        RLock lock = redisson.getLock("lock:payment:" + orderId);
        boolean acquired = false;
        try {
            // Wait up to 5s to acquire, auto-release after 30s
            acquired = lock.tryLock(5, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            if (!acquired) {
                throw new PaymentConflictException(
                    "Another instance is processing order " + orderId);
            }

            // Critical section — only one JVM executes this at a time
            return doProcessPayment(orderId, amount);

        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            throw new PaymentException("Lock acquisition interrupted", e);
        } finally {
            // Only unlock if we actually hold the lock
            if (acquired && lock.isHeldByCurrentThread()) {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Key parameters:** The wait time (5s) bounds how long a thread will block waiting for a contended lock. The lease time (30s) caps how long the lock is held — if the critical section takes longer, the lock expires and another instance can proceed. Use Redisson's watchdog (omit lease time) to auto-extend while the thread is alive.

Common pitfalls and how to avoid each one:

**Pitfall 1 — Forgetting to release in `finally`:**
If an exception occurs inside the critical section and you do not release in `finally`, the lock stays held until the lease expires, blocking all other instances.

**Pitfall 2 — Blind `unlock()` without ownership check:**
```java
// WRONG — throws IllegalMonitorStateException if lock expired
lock.unlock();

// CORRECT — verify current thread still owns the lock
if (lock.isHeldByCurrentThread()) {
    lock.unlock();
}
```
If your critical section runs longer than the lease, the lock expires and another thread acquires it. Calling `unlock()` on a lock you no longer own releases someone else's lock.

**Pitfall 3 — Lease time shorter than critical section:**
Set lease time with headroom (2–3× expected execution time), or omit it to enable the watchdog, which extends the lease every 10 seconds while the thread is alive.

**Pitfall 4 — Single Redis node for strong guarantees:**
A single Redis node is a single point of failure. If Redis restarts, all locks vanish. For critical paths, use `RedissonMultiLock` across 3+ independent Redis instances (RedLock algorithm).

**Pitfall 5 — No fencing token for downstream writes:**
Even with distributed locks, a slow thread can lose the lock and still complete its write. Use a monotonically increasing fencing token to let downstream services reject stale operations.

Spring Boot service wrapping the fixed-window Lua script:

```java
@Service
public class RateLimiter {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final RedisScript<Long> script;

    public RateLimiter(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
        this.script = RedisScript.of(
            new ClassPathResource("scripts/rate_limit_fixed.lua"), Long.class);
    }

    public boolean isAllowed(String clientId, int maxRequests, int windowSeconds) {
        String key = "ratelimit:" + clientId + ":"
            + Instant.now().getEpochSecond() / windowSeconds;
        Long result = redis.execute(script,
            List.of(key),
            String.valueOf(maxRequests),
            String.valueOf(windowSeconds));
        return result != null && result == 1;
    }
}
```

