Redis Pub/Sub implementation in Spring Boot:

```java
@Configuration
public class RedisPubSubConfig {

    @Bean
    public RedisMessageListenerContainer listenerContainer(
            RedisConnectionFactory connectionFactory) {
        RedisMessageListenerContainer container =
            new RedisMessageListenerContainer();
        container.setConnectionFactory(connectionFactory);
        container.addMessageListener(
            cacheInvalidationListener(),
            new ChannelTopic("cache:invalidation"));
        return container;
    }

    @Bean
    public MessageListenerAdapter cacheInvalidationListener() {
        return new MessageListenerAdapter(new CacheInvalidationHandler());
    }
}
```

```java
public class CacheInvalidationHandler {

    @Autowired
    private CacheManager localCacheManager;

    // Called whenever a message arrives on the "cache:invalidation" channel
    public void handleMessage(String message) {
        String[] parts = message.split(":");
        String cacheName = parts[0];
        String key = parts[1];
        Cache cache = localCacheManager.getCache(cacheName);
        if (cache != null) {
            cache.evict(key);
        }
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    @Autowired
    private StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public void updateProduct(Product product) {
        productRepo.save(product);
        // Broadcast invalidation to all app instances
        redis.convertAndSend("cache:invalidation",
            "products:" + product.getId());
    }
}
```

**Key insight:** This pattern is ideal for multi-instance cache invalidation. When one instance updates a product, all instances evict their local Caffeine cache entry. If an instance is offline during the broadcast, it misses the message — which is acceptable because the local cache entry will eventually expire via TTL.

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    @Autowired
    private RedisTemplate<String, Product> redisTemplate;
    @Autowired
    private ProductRepository productRepo;

    // VULNERABLE: No stampede protection
    public Product getProduct(Long id) {
        String key = "product:" + id;
        Product cached = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get(key);
        if (cached != null) {
            return cached;
        }
        // 10,000 concurrent requests ALL reach here simultaneously
        // when the cache entry expires
        Product product = productRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        redisTemplate.opsForValue().set(key, product, Duration.ofMinutes(10));
        return product;
    }
}
```

This code works perfectly under normal load. The problem only manifests when a hot key expires under high concurrency — every thread independently detects the miss and hits the database.

```java
@Service
public class StampedeProtectedProductService {

    private final RedisTemplate<String, Product> redis;
    private final RedissonClient redisson;
    private final ProductRepository repo;

    public Product getProduct(Long id) {
        String cacheKey = "product:" + id;
        Product cached = redis.opsForValue().get(cacheKey);
        if (cached != null) {
            return cached;
        }

        // Mutex: only ONE thread refreshes the cache
        RLock lock = redisson.getLock("lock:refresh:" + cacheKey);
        try {
            if (lock.tryLock(3, 10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                try {
                    // Double-check: another thread may have refreshed
                    cached = redis.opsForValue().get(cacheKey);
                    if (cached != null) {
                        return cached;
                    }
                    Product product = repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
                    redis.opsForValue().set(
                        cacheKey, product, Duration.ofMinutes(10));
                    return product;
                } finally {
                    if (lock.isHeldByCurrentThread()) lock.unlock();
                }
            }
            // Lock contention — wait briefly and retry from cache
            Thread.sleep(100);
            return redis.opsForValue().get(cacheKey);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            return repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        }
    }
}
```

The double-check after acquiring the lock is essential — by the time thread #2 acquires the lock, thread #1 has already repopulated the cache.

```java
@Service
public class PERCacheService {

    private final RedisTemplate<String, String> redis;
    private final ProductRepository repo;
    private static final double BETA = 1.0; // Aggressiveness of early refresh

    public Product getProduct(Long id) {
        String key = "product:" + id;
        String raw = redis.opsForValue().get(key);
        if (raw == null) {
            return refreshAndCache(id, key);
        }

        CachedEntry entry = deserialize(raw);

        // PER formula: should we proactively refresh?
        long ttl = redis.getExpire(key, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
        long delta = entry.getComputeTimeMs(); // How long DB query took
        double random = -delta * BETA * Math.log(Math.random());
        if (random >= ttl) {
            // Probabilistically trigger early refresh
            return refreshAndCache(id, key);
        }
        return entry.getProduct();
    }

    private Product refreshAndCache(Long id, String key) {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        Product product = repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        long computeTime = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
        CachedEntry entry = new CachedEntry(product, computeTime);
        redis.opsForValue().set(key, serialize(entry), Duration.ofMinutes(10));
        return product;
    }
}
```

**How PER works:** As TTL approaches zero, the probability of triggering an early refresh increases exponentially. With high concurrency, one request statistically refreshes early — before the actual expiry — so the key never truly expires. The `BETA` parameter controls aggressiveness: higher values cause earlier refreshes. This approach requires no locking and no coordination between threads.

Event-driven invalidation using Spring's cache annotations:

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    // Read path: cache the result
    @Cacheable(value = "products", key = "#id")
    public Product findById(Long id) {
        return productRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    }

    // Write path: evict the stale entry after update
    @CacheEvict(value = "products", key = "#product.id")
    public Product update(Product product) {
        return productRepo.save(product);
    }

    // Cascading invalidation: category change affects many products
    @Caching(evict = {
        @CacheEvict(value = "categories", key = "#id"),
        @CacheEvict(value = "products", allEntries = true)
    })
    public void updateCategory(Long id, Category category) {
        categoryRepo.save(category);
    }

    // Pattern-based eviction using RedisTemplate directly
    public void evictProductsByPrefix(String prefix) {
        Set<String> keys = redisTemplate.keys("products:" + prefix + "*");
        if (keys != null && !keys.isEmpty()) {
            redisTemplate.delete(keys);
        }
    }
}
```

**Warning:** `redisTemplate.keys()` uses the `KEYS` command, which blocks the Redis event loop on large keyspaces. For production, use `SCAN` via `RedisTemplate.execute()` with a `ScanOptions` cursor instead.

Multi-instance cache sync using Redis Pub/Sub:

```java
@Configuration
public class CacheInvalidationConfig {

    public static final String INVALIDATION_CHANNEL = "cache:invalidate";

    @Bean
    public RedisMessageListenerContainer listenerContainer(
            RedisConnectionFactory cf) {
        RedisMessageListenerContainer container =
            new RedisMessageListenerContainer();
        container.setConnectionFactory(cf);
        container.addMessageListener(
            new CacheInvalidationListener(localCacheManager()),
            new ChannelTopic(INVALIDATION_CHANNEL));
        return container;
    }
}
```

```java
public class CacheInvalidationListener implements MessageListener {

    private final CacheManager localCache;

    @Override
    public void onMessage(Message message, byte[] pattern) {
        String payload = new String(message.getBody());
        // payload format: "cacheName:key"
        String[] parts = payload.split(":", 2);
        Cache cache = localCache.getCache(parts[0]);
        if (cache != null) {
            cache.evict(parts[1]);
        }
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class UserService {

    @Autowired private StringRedisTemplate redis;

    @CacheEvict(value = "users", key = "#user.id")
    public User updateUser(User user) {
        User saved = userRepo.save(user);
        // Broadcast to all instances so they evict local Caffeine cache
        redis.convertAndSend(
            CacheInvalidationConfig.INVALIDATION_CHANNEL,
            "users:" + saved.getId());
        return saved;
    }
}
```

This pattern ensures that when instance A updates a user, instances B and C immediately evict their local Caffeine cache entries. The Redis cache is evicted by `@CacheEvict`, and the pub/sub broadcast handles the local caches.

Create a configuration class to customize session behavior:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableRedisHttpSession(
    maxInactiveIntervalInSeconds = 1800, // 30 minutes
    redisNamespace = "myapp:session",     // Key prefix in Redis
    flushMode = FlushMode.ON_SAVE         // Batch writes until response commit
)
public class SessionConfig {

    @Bean
    public CookieSerializer cookieSerializer() {
        DefaultCookieSerializer serializer = new DefaultCookieSerializer();
        serializer.setCookieName("SESSIONID");
        serializer.setDomainNamePattern("^.+?(\\.[\\w]+\\.[\\w]+)$");
        serializer.setSameSite("Lax");
        serializer.setUseSecureCookie(true);
        return serializer;
    }

    // For REST APIs: propagate session ID via X-Auth-Token header
    @Bean
    public HttpSessionIdResolver httpSessionIdResolver() {
        return HeaderHttpSessionIdResolver.xAuthToken();
    }
}
```

The `redisNamespace` prevents key collisions between environments or applications sharing the same Redis instance.

Use `HttpSession` exactly as before — Spring Session transparently redirects to Redis:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class CartController {

    @PostMapping("/cart/add")
    public ResponseEntity<Cart> addToCart(
            HttpSession session,
            @RequestBody CartItem item) {
        // getAttribute/setAttribute work exactly like in-memory sessions
        Cart cart = (Cart) session.getAttribute("cart");
        if (cart == null) {
            cart = new Cart();
        }
        cart.addItem(item);
        session.setAttribute("cart", cart); // Persisted to Redis
        return ResponseEntity.ok(cart);
    }

    @GetMapping("/cart")
    public ResponseEntity<Cart> getCart(HttpSession session) {
        Cart cart = (Cart) session.getAttribute("cart");
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
            cart != null ? cart : new Cart());
    }

    @PostMapping("/logout")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> logout(HttpSession session) {
        session.invalidate(); // Removes from Redis immediately
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }
}
```

**Important:** Every object stored via `setAttribute` must implement `Serializable`. For cleaner Redis storage, configure a JSON serializer on the `RedisTemplate` used by Spring Session. Keep session payloads small — every request reads and writes the full session hash.

Memory-efficient patterns for Java/Spring Boot applications:

```java
@Configuration
public class RedisMemoryConfig {

    @Bean
    public RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory cf) {
        // Use JSON serializer (2-3× smaller than JDK serialization)
        GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer jsonSerializer =
            new GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer(
                new ObjectMapper()
                    .setSerializationInclusion(JsonInclude.Include.NON_NULL)
                    .disable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS));

        // Short key prefixes save memory at scale
        RedisCacheConfiguration config = RedisCacheConfiguration
            .defaultCacheConfig()
            .computePrefixWith(name -> name + ":")
            .serializeValuesWith(
                SerializationPair.fromSerializer(jsonSerializer))
            .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(15));

        return RedisCacheManager.builder(cf)
            .cacheDefaults(config)
            .build();
    }
}
```

**Key memory optimization tips:**

1. **Use Hash instead of separate Strings** — One Hash with 20 fields uses ~10× less RAM than 20 String keys due to per-key overhead (~70 bytes each) and listpack encoding.

2. **Set TTL on every key** — Keys without TTL accumulate forever. Even if you use `@CacheEvict`, bugs or missed events can orphan entries.

3. **Shorten key names at scale** — `u:42:p` instead of `user:42:profile` saves bytes across millions of keys.

4. **Avoid storing large blobs** — Multi-MB values spike latency and are expensive to evict. Use an object store (S3) for payloads and Redis for metadata.

5. **Monitor `OBJECT ENCODING`** — If a Hash shows `hashtable` instead of `listpack`, it exceeded the threshold and uses significantly more memory.
